    {
      "location": "Raw PDF page 23, printed page 1, introduction surrounding (1.1)",
      "anchor": "q : ℝ × ℝ → ℝᵐ; subscripts denote partial derivatives",
      "observation": "The inherited setting has one spatial coordinate x, time t, and an m-component real-valued state q. The components represent unknown physical quantities."
    },
    {
      "location": "Raw PDF page 25, printed page 3, Section 1.1, (1.8)–(1.9)",
      "anchor": "q_t(x,t) + f(q(x,t))_x = 0",
      "observation": "The differential conservation law is (1.8), with f called the flux function. Its quasilinear rewriting uses the flux Jacobian f′(q)."
    },
    {
      "location": "Raw PDF page 26, printed page 4, continuation of Section 1.1",
      "anchor": "linear flux function f(q) = Aq; q, a vector of conserved quantities",
      "observation": "The context includes linear and nonlinear flux functions and vector-valued conserved states."
    },
    {
      "location": "Raw PDF page 26, printed page 4, Section 1.1.1, (1.10)",
      "anchor": "for any two points x₁ and x₂",
      "observation": "The integral balance is d/dt ∫ from x₁ to x₂ q(x,t) dx = f(q(x₁,t)) − f(q(x₂,t))."
    },
    {
      "location": "Raw PDF page 26, printed page 4, paragraph following (1.10)",
      "anchor": "Each component of q measures the density of some conserved quantity",
      "observation": "The integral measures the total amount of each conserved quantity between the endpoints; that amount changes only through endpoint fluxes. Nonconservation requires source terms."
    },
    {
      "location": "Raw PDF page 26, printed page 4, Section 1.1.2, first paragraph",
      "anchor": "provided that q and f(q) are sufficiently smooth",
      "observation": "The differential equation (1.8) can be derived from the integral equation (1.10) under sufficient smoothness of both q and the composed flux f(q). The source refers to Chapter 2 for the manipulations."
    },
    {
      "location": "Raw PDF pages 26–27, printed pages 4–5, Section 1.1.2, paragraph spanning the page break",
      "anchor": "discontinuities such as shock waves; even from smooth initial data",
      "observation": "Many interesting solutions contain discontinuities. Nonlinear conservation laws can develop such discontinuities spontaneously from smooth initial data."
    },
    {
      "location": "Raw PDF page 27, printed page 5, Section 1.1.2, second paragraph",
      "anchor": "At a discontinuity in q ... does not hold in the classical sense",
      "observation": "At a discontinuity in q, the differential conservation law (1.8) does not hold classically, while the integral conservation law (1.10) remains the more fundamental equation and continues to hold. Shock-wave solution theory is deferred to Chapter 11."
    },
    {
      "location": "Raw PDF page 27, printed page 5, Section 1.2, first paragraph",
      "anchor": "finite volume methods ... based on the integral form (1.10)",
      "observation": "The ensuing numerical discussion uses integrals or cell averages and endpoint fluxes to approximate discontinuous solutions."
    }
  ],
  "statement": {
    "binders": [
      "x and t are the spatial and temporal variables in a one-dimensional, time-dependent problem; q(x,t) takes values in ℝᵐ. [Printed p. 1, introduction surrounding (1.1).]",
      "f is the flux function acting on the conserved state q, with vector output compatible with the conservation law. Its precise domain is not separately specified here. [Printed pp. 3–4, (1.8)–(1.9) and the continuation describing f(q).]",
      "x₁ and x₂ are arbitrary spatial endpoints; they appear as fixed limits in the time-differentiated integral. [Printed p. 4, Section 1.1.1, 'for any two points' and (1.10).]"
    ],
    "hypotheses": [
      "The setting is a homogeneous conservation law for densities of conserved quantities, with differential form (1.8) and integral form (1.10). [Printed pp. 3–4, Section 1.1 and Section 1.1.1.]",
      "The stated derivation of (1.8) from (1.10) assumes that q and f(q) are sufficiently smooth. [Printed p. 4, Section 1.1.2, first sentence.]",
      "The assertion of classical failure concerns a discontinuity in the solution q. [Printed p. 5, Section 1.1.2, second paragraph.]",
      "The spontaneous development assertion specifically concerns nonlinear conservation laws and allows smooth initial data. [Printed pp. 4–5, Section 1.1.2, paragraph spanning the page break.]"
    ],
    "conclusions": [
      "Under the stated sufficient smoothness, the integral balance (1.10) yields q_t(x,t) + ∂_x(f(q(x,t))) = 0, equation (1.8). [Printed p. 4, Section 1.1.2, first sentence.]",
      "At a discontinuity in q, equation (1.8) does not hold in the classical sense. [Printed p. 5, Section 1.1.2, second paragraph.]",
      "For the discontinuous conservation-law solutions under discussion, the integral conservation law continues to hold: d/dt ∫ from x₁ to x₂ q(x,t) dx = f(q(x₁,t)) − f(q(x₂,t)). The source treats this integral law as more fundamental. [Printed p. 4, (1.10); printed p. 5, Section 1.1.2, second paragraph.]",
      "Nonlinear conservation laws can develop discontinuities spontaneously even from smooth initial data; the statement expresses a possibility, not inevitability for every nonlinear law or every smooth datum. [Printed pp. 4–5, Section 1.1.2, 'can easily develop spontaneously even from smooth initial data'.]"
    ],
    "definitions_and_conventions": [
      "The subscripts t and x denote partial derivatives; in (1.8), the x derivative applies to the composed flux f(q(x,t)). [Printed p. 1, explanation following (1.1); printed p. 3, (1.8).]",
      "Each component of q is a density, and its spatial integral gives the total conserved quantity between the endpoints. [Printed p. 4, paragraph following (1.10).]",
      "The endpoint flux difference is f(q(x₁,t)) minus f(q(x₂,t)), in that order. [Printed p. 4, (1.10).]",
      "Equation (1.10) has no source term; the source explains that failure of physical conservation requires adding source terms. [Printed p. 4, paragraph following (1.10).]"
    ],
    "implicit_context": [
      "The discontinuity passage discusses solutions of conservation laws, not unrestricted discontinuous functions. This scope is inherited from Section 1.1 and the reference to shock-wave solutions in Section 1.1.2. [Printed pp. 3–5, Sections 1.1–1.1.2.]",
      "The surrounding discussion covers systems as well as scalar equations; there is no scalar-only restriction in the selected passage. [Printed p. 1, q : ℝ × ℝ → ℝᵐ; printed p. 4, 'Each component of q'.]",
      "Detailed derivations and shock-wave solution theory are deferred respectively to Chapter 2 and Chapter 11; the supplied pages give an introductory statement rather than a complete regularity theorem. [Printed p. 4, Section 1.1.2, first sentence; printed p. 5, Section 1.1.2, final sentence.]"
    ],
    "semantic_facets": [
      {
        "topic": "Classical versus integral validity",
        "details": [
          "Classical failure is localized by the phrase 'At a discontinuity in q'; the passage does not assert failure at every point of the solution. [Printed p. 5, Section 1.1.2, second paragraph.]",
          "Integral validity is a conservation balance over spatial intervals and is not asserted as a consequence of discontinuity alone. [Printed p. 4, (1.10) and its conservation interpretation; printed p. 5, Section 1.1.2.]"
        ]
      },
      {
        "topic": "Regularity and derivation direction",
        "details": [
          "The explicit derivation runs from integral form to differential form under sufficient smoothness of q and f(q). [Printed p. 4, Section 1.1.2, first sentence.]",
